  "hypothesis": "At B=1, 32 query heads, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128 and a 32k cache, one decode step streams 128 MiB of K and V. Against the analytic 3.30 TB/s floor (0.0407 ms) the measured 0.0636 ms looks like 1.56x of headroom, so a tiling/scheduling rewrite of the attention kernel is claimed to recover a large part of it.\n",
  "mechanism": {
    "name": "retile the decode attention kernel to stream K/V at the read ceiling",
    "class": "tiling",
    "justification": "Attention reads exactly the bytes the ceiling kernel reads, so the only reachable saving is the gap between measured latency and the time a same-bytes read takes. That gap is 2.18 us at 32k context, 3.4 % of the operator. No byte can be removed: KV cache is dense and fully consumed.\n"
  },
